  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "T.Manoharan vs V.Sudheendran on 1 February, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Monday, The 1St Day Of February 2016/12Th Magha, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 4049 of 2009 ( ) --------------------------------- Crl.A 540/2007 of Sessions Court,Thiruvananthapuram Cc 88/1992 of Addl.C.J.M.,Tthiruvananthapuram Revision Petitioner(S)/2Nd Accused/Appellant.: --------------------------------------------------------------------------- T.Manoharan, S/O. S.Thangadurai, Partner, M/S. T.Manoharan & Co., Door No.191 East Masi Street, Madurai, Tamil Nadu. By Advs.Sri.Nagaraj Narayanan Sri.Saijo Hassan Sri.A.S.Sabu Sri.Prathap Pillai Respondent(S)/Complainat, 1St Accused/Respondets & State: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- 1. V.Sudheendran, Food Inspector, Corporation Of Thiruvananthapuram. 2. K.Sivadas, T.C.39/291, Kumaresan Provision Store, Kothukal Street, Chalai Thiruvananthapuram. 3. State Of Kerala, Represented By The Public Prosecutor High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam. By Public Prosecutor, Shri.V.S.Sreejith This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 01-02-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: T.Manoharan vs V.Sudheendran on 1 February, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/39741097/ 1 B.Sudheendra Kumar, J. - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - -- - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - Crl.R.P. No.4049 of 2009 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - Dated this the 1st day of February 2016 O R D E R",
    "facts": "The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 26.10.1991 at about 2 p.m., PW1, the Food Inspector, purchased 750 gram of pepper from the shop of the first accused. Second accused was the distributor. After sampling as per rules, one of the samples was sent for analysis to the Public Analyst. The Public Analyst filed Ext. P10 report stating that the sample was coated with diesel and therefore, the same was adulterated. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW5 were examined and Exts.P1 to P15 were marked for the prosecution. DW1 to DW3 were examined and Exts.D1 to D6 were marked for the revision petitioner.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P10 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P10 report of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra). It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled T.Manoharan vs V.Sudheendran on 1 February, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/39741097/ 2 to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P10 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P10 report of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra).",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled T.Manoharan vs V.Sudheendran on 1 February, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/39741097/ 2 to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused."
  },
